<!--
  sandbox-network.xml — the isolated libvirt network the detonation guest
  lives on. Defined by `kvm_manage.sh net-setup`.

  ┌─ WHY THERE IS NO <forward> ELEMENT ───────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Its absence is the isolation. With no <forward>, libvirt creates the  │
  │ bridge and runs dnsmasq on it, but installs no NAT and no forwarding  │
  │ rule — the guest can reach the host bridge address and nothing else.  │
  │ Adding <forward mode='nat'/> here would give live malware a route to  │
  │ the internet from inside your network. Do not add it "temporarily".   │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Verify before the first detonation, and after any libvirt or host
  firewall change:

    virsh net-dumpxml sandbox | grep -c forward   # must print 0
    iptables -S FORWARD | grep virbr-sandbox      # must show the DROP pair

  And from inside the guest, which is the check that actually counts:

    ping -n 1 1.1.1.1     # must fail
    nslookup example.com  # must resolve to the INetSim address, not the truth

  IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §Phase 0 pairs this with belt-and-braces rules
  that do not depend on libvirt getting it right:

    iptables -I FORWARD -i virbr-sandbox -o <wan-if> -j DROP
    iptables -I FORWARD -i <wan-if> -o virbr-sandbox -j DROP
-->
<network xmlns:dnsmasq='http://libvirt.org/schemas/network/dnsmasq/1.0'>
  <name>sandbox</name>
  <bridge name='virbr-sandbox' stp='on' delay='0'/>

  <!-- Zeek and Suricata sniff this bridge from the host (Phase 4), so it
       has to be a normal Linux bridge with a host-side address — not an
       <forward mode='none'/> isolated port group. -->
  <ip address='10.10.10.254' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
    <dhcp>
      <!-- A one-address pool. The guest's MAC is fixed in win11-kvm.xml and
           pinned here, so VM_HOST=10.10.10.2 in the worker environment is
           always right and the orchestrator never has to discover a lease.
           A second guest on this network would fail to get an address,
           which is the correct outcome: two samples detonating on one
           bridge would contaminate each other's network evidence. -->
      <range start='10.10.10.2' end='10.10.10.2'/>
      <host mac='00:1a:a0:3c:4d:5e' name='win11-sandbox' ip='10.10.10.2'/>
    </dhcp>
  </ip>

  <!-- DNS goes straight from the guest to INetSim. Every lookup is answered
       with INetSim's own address, which is what keeps a sample talking: a
       lookup that fails ends the conversation and the report says nothing.

       ┌─ WHY NOT <dns><forwarder addr='10.10.10.1'/></dns> ────────────────┐
       │ That was the earlier shape, and it cannot work here. It hands the  │
       │ guest 10.10.10.254 as its resolver and asks libvirt's dnsmasq to   │
       │ forward to INetSim. dnsmasq runs on the *host*, and INetSim is a   │
       │ macvlan container — a host cannot talk to a macvlan child of its   │
       │ own parent interface. docker-compose.sandbox.yml says so outright: │
       │ "the host itself cannot talk to these containers." So every query  │
       │ would SERVFAIL and every sample would go quiet.                    │
       │                                                                    │
       │ dhcp-option=6 replaces the resolver rather than chaining to it, so │
       │ the guest queries 10.10.10.1 directly and the host is not in the   │
       │ path at all. One less hop and one less thing to be running.        │
       └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

       There is still no upstream resolver anywhere, so no path by which a
       real name could resolve even if the FORWARD rules were removed. If
       INetSim is down the guest gets no answer — the correct failure, and a
       visible one.

       Verify from inside the guest, which is the check that counts:
         nslookup example.com    # must answer 10.10.10.1, not the truth -->
  <domain name='sandbox.local'/>
  <dnsmasq:options>
    <dnsmasq:option value='dhcp-option=6,10.10.10.1'/>
  </dnsmasq:options>
</network>
